course: dictionary
lesson: 12026
title: Chomp: noisy biting and eating
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like what they mean, and chomp is one of them: say it out loud and you can almost hear teeth crunching. To chomp means to bite and chew food noisily, with big mouthfuls. «The dog chomped its bone with big, happy bites.» The clues are big bites and happy, so the dog is eating loudly and eagerly.
explain: Chomp can also be a noun, meaning a big, noisy bite. «Tom took a huge chomp out of his apple.» Here chomp is the bite itself, not the action. Ask yourself: is it a thing or a doing word? Try both meanings in the sentence and see which one makes sense.
explain: Chomp is often used with small words. «Chomp on» means keep eating, and «chomp through» means eat a lot of something quickly. «Chomping at the bit» has nothing to do with food: it means very eager to start. «The runners were chomping at the bit before the whistle.»
explain: Context clues help you work out the meaning. Food, bites and mouths point to eating, while words like whistle or ready point to the eager meaning. When you are not sure, swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still sounds right.
fact: Chomp means to bite and chew food noisily, with big mouthfuls.
fact: As a noun, a chomp is a big, noisy bite.
fact: «Chomp through» means to eat a lot of something quickly.
fact: «Chomping at the bit» means being very eager to start.
fact: Chomp is a verb (chomp, chomps, chomped, chomping) and can also be a noun.
exercise: «The crocodile chomped the fish in one big bite.» What does chomped mean? | Swam quietly under the water | Caught a cold | *Bit and chewed noisily | Slept in the sun
exercise: «Tom took a huge chomp out of his apple.» In this sentence, what is a chomp? | A verb meaning to sleep | A colour | *A big, noisy bite | A place to sit
exercise: «The students were chomping at the bit for the bell.» What does chomping at the bit mean? | *Very eager to start | Bored and tired of school | Feeling sick after lunch | Chewing their pencils
test: «Dad chomped through a whole pizza before the film started.» What does chomped through mean? | *Ate a lot of something quickly | Put it in the fridge | Dropped it on the floor | Shared it with the dog
test: Ruby says chomp can only be a doing word. Is she right? | Yes, it can only ever be a doing word | *No, a chomp can also be a noun: a big, noisy bite | Yes, because it has an s on the end | No, it is a colour
exam: «The horse chomped on its hay while the rain fell.» What does chomped on mean here? | Stopped eating for a rest | Shook off the rain | Sang a happy tune | *Kept eating noisily
